    "id": "cp_ds_arrays_2",
    "title": "Delivery Route Segment Counter",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "topic_id": "ds_arrays",
    "language": "python",
    "problem_statement": "## Problem\n\nA logistics company tracks fuel consumption per road segment as an array of integers (can be negative if a segment is downhill and recovers fuel). A **delivery route** is a contiguous subarray. The company wants to know how many distinct delivery routes have a total fuel cost **exactly equal to** a given target `k`.\n\nGiven an integer array `fuel` and an integer `k`, return the **count of contiguous subarrays** whose sum equals `k`.\n\n### Function Signature\n```python\ndef count_routes(fuel: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n```\n\n### Input\n- First line: space-separated integers representing `fuel`.\n- Second line: integer `k`.\n\n### Output\n- A single integer: the number of subarrays with sum equal to `k`.",
    "constraints": {
      "n_min": 1,
      "n_max": 100000,
      "value_min": -1000,
      "value_max": 1000,
      "k_min": -1000000,
      "k_max": 1000000,
      "time_limit": "O(n)",
      "space_limit": "O(n)"
    },
    "examples": [
      {
        "input": "fuel = [1, 2, 3], k = 3",
        "output": "2",
        "explanation": "The subarrays [1,2] (sum=3) and [3] (sum=3) both equal k, giving a count of 2."
      },
      {
        "input": "fuel = [1, -1, 1], k = 1",
        "output": "3",
        "explanation": "Valid subarrays are [1] at index 0 (sum=1), [1,-1,1] (sum=1), and [1] at index 2 (sum=1), totalling 3."
      }
    ],
    "solution": {
      "brute_force": {
        "approach": "Check every pair (i, j) and compute the subarray sum using a nested loop. This is O(n²) and will time out for n=100000, but is useful to verify correctness on small inputs.",
        "code": "def count_routes_brute(fuel, k):\n    n = len(fuel)\n    count = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        total = 0\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            total += fuel[j]\n            if total == k:\n                count += 1\n    return count",
        "time": "O(n^2)",
        "space": "O(1)"
      },
      "optimal": {
        "approach": "Use prefix sum + hashmap. At each index, the running sum is `curr`. If `curr - k` was seen before (stored in freq map), those prior prefixes form subarrays ending here with sum k. Pre-seed freq={0:1} to handle subarrays starting at index 0.",
        "code": "def count_routes(fuel, k):\n    freq = {0: 1}   # empty prefix has sum 0\n    curr = 0\n    count = 0\n\n    for val in fuel:\n        curr += val\n        # subarrays ending here with sum k exist if (curr - k) was seen before\n        count += freq.get(curr - k, 0)\n        freq[curr] = freq.get(curr, 0) + 1\n\n    return count",
        "time": "O(n)",
        "space": "O(n)"
      }
    },
    "test_cases": [
      {
        "input": "fuel = [1, 2, 3], k = 3",
        "expected": "2",
        "is_visible": true
      },
      {
        "input": "fuel = [1, -1, 1], k = 1",
        "expected": "3",
        "is_visible": true
      },
      {
        "input": "fuel = [5], k = 5",
        "expected": "1",
        "is_visible": false
      },
