        "input": "fuel = [-1, -1, 1, 1, 1], k = 0",
        "expected": "3",
        "is_visible": false
      },
      {
        "input": "fuel = [3, -3, 3, -3, 3], k = 3",
        "expected": "5",
        "is_visible": false
      }
    ],
    "company_tags": ["Amazon", "TCS"],
    "round_type": "phone_screen",
    "hints": [
      "A sliding window won't work here because the array contains negative numbers — shrinking the window can actually increase the sum.",
      "Think about prefix sums: if prefix[j] - prefix[i] = k, then the subarray from i+1 to j has sum k. How can you count pairs efficiently?",
      "Use a hashmap to store frequencies of prefix sums seen so far. For each new prefix sum `curr`, add freq[curr - k] to the answer, then record curr in the map."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "cp_ds_arrays_3",
    "title": "Signal Broadcast Window",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "topic_id": "ds_arrays",
    "language": "python",
    "problem_statement": "## Problem\n\nA radio tower broadcasts signals over a string `signal` of lowercase letters. An engineer wants to find the length of the **longest contiguous broadcast segment** that contains **at most `k` distinct frequency channels** (distinct characters).\n\nGiven a string `signal` and an integer `k`, return the length of the longest substring with at most `k` distinct characters. If `k` is 0 or the string is empty, return 0.\n\n### Function Signature\n```python\ndef longest_broadcast(signal: str, k: int) -> int:\n```\n\n### Input\n- First line: string `signal` of lowercase English letters.\n- Second line: integer `k`.\n\n### Output\n- A single integer: the length of the longest valid substring.",
    "constraints": {
      "n_min": 1,
      "n_max": 100000,
      "k_min": 0,
      "k_max": 26,
      "characters": "lowercase English letters a-z",
      "time_limit": "O(n)",
      "space_limit": "O(k)"
    },
    "examples": [
      {
        "input": "signal = \"eceba\", k = 2",
        "output": "3",
        "explanation": "The substring \"ece\" has 2 distinct characters and length 3, which is the longest valid window."
      },
      {
        "input": "signal = \"aabbcc\", k = 1",
        "output": "2",
        "explanation": "The longest windows with exactly 1 distinct character are \"aa\" and \"bb\" and \"cc\", each of length 2."
      }
    ],
    "solution": {
      "brute_force": {
        "approach": "Check all O(n²) substrings and for each compute the number of distinct characters using a set. Return the length of the longest one with at most k distinct characters. This is O(n²) and too slow for n=100000.",
        "code": "def longest_broadcast_brute(signal, k):\n    if k == 0:\n        return 0\n    n = len(signal)\n    best = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        seen = set()\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            seen.add(signal[j])\n            if len(seen) <= k:\n                best = max(best, j - i + 1)\n            else:\n                break\n    return best",
        "time": "O(n^2)",
        "space": "O(k)"
      },
      "optimal": {
        "approach": "Variable sliding window with a frequency hashmap. Expand right pointer and add the character to the map. When the number of distinct characters exceeds k, shrink from the left until the window is valid again. Track the maximum window size throughout.",
        "code": "def longest_broadcast(signal, k):\n    if k == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    freq = {}   # character -> count within current window\n    left = 0\n    best = 0\n\n    for right in range(len(signal)):\n        ch = signal[right]\n        freq[ch] = freq.get(ch, 0) + 1  # expand window\n\n        # shrink from left until at most k distinct chars\n        while len(freq) > k:\n            left_ch = signal[left]\n            freq[left_ch] -= 1\n            if freq[left_ch] == 0:\n                del freq[left_ch]   # remove character from window entirely\n            left += 1\n\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n\n    return best",
        "time": "O(n)",
        "space": "O(k)"
      }
    },
    "test_cases": [
      {
        "input": "signal = \"eceba\", k = 2",
        "expected": "3",
        "is_visible": true
      },
      {
        "input": "signal = \"aabbcc\", k = 1",
        "expected": "2",
        "is_visible": true
      },
      {
        "input": "signal = \"abcde\", k = 0",
        "expected": "0",
        "is_visible": false
      },
      {
        "input": "signal = \"aaaa\", k = 3",
        "expected": "4",
        "is_visible": false
      },
      {
        "input": "signal = \"abaccc\", k = 2",
        "expected": "4",
        "is_visible": false
      },
      {
        "input": "signal = \"a\", k = 1",
        "expected": "1",
        "is_visible": false
      }
    ],
    "company_tags": ["Amazon"],
    "round_type": "on_site",
    "hints": [
      "A brute-force checks all O(n²) substrings — think about whether you can maintain a window that you only ever grow or shrink from one end at a time.",
      "Use a sliding window: expand the right pointer to include new characters, and shrink the left pointer whenever the number of distinct characters exceeds k.",
      "Keep a frequency dictionary of characters in the current window. The number of keys in that dictionary tells you how many distinct characters are present. When it exceeds k, remove characters from the left until it is valid again."
    ]
  }
]

